diagnosis_text:
The reported "black hole at the top-most box" is not occurring. A thorough inspection of every device in the dc_clos_bgp fabric — all five routers and both hosts — reveals a fully healthy network with end-to-end connectivity confirmed by 0% packet-loss ping and traceroute.

The actual forwarding path for traffic from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1. The super_spine_router_0 does not appear in this path at all. In this three-tier eBGP Clos design, super_spine_router_0 serves purely as a BGP route relay between the two spine routers, providing backup paths if a spine-to-leaf peering fails. It is not in the primary forwarding plane for intra-fabric leaf-to-leaf traffic.

The detail that may have confused the field technician is that spine_router_0_0 reports receiving zero prefixes from super_spine_router_0 (PfxRcd=0). This is entirely correct behavior: super_spine's best-path routes for both host subnets were originally learned from spine_router_0_0 (AS 65100), so when super_spine re-advertises them, spine_router_0_0 sees its own AS number in the AS-PATH and rejects them via BGP's standard loop-prevention mechanism. The routes are correctly accepted by spine_router_0_1 as longer-path backups. All eight BGP sessions across the fabric are in Established state, all kernel FIBs are correct, and there are no null routes, ACLs, or route-maps anywhere.

No corrective action is required. The reported symptom either was transient (a brief BGP reconvergence at lab startup) or was a misidentification; the network is healthy as of this investigation.
=== END ===